(verb :name "hash-table-pop!" :std "SRFI-125" :library "hash" :tier "core" :kind "mut" :signature "(hash-table-pop! h) -> two values: key and value"
  :summary "Remove one association from h and return TWO values, its key and its value. Popping an empty table is an error, so guard with hash-table-empty? first. SRFI-125."
  :explanation "The take-one-out verb, and the only verb in this library that returns two values. It does not take a key — it chooses one for you, removes it, and hands back both halves of the association, so a single call does what hash-table-keys, car, hash-table-ref and hash-table-delete! would take four calls to do. Consume the two values with let-values or define-values. The table must not be empty: there is no association to return and no sensible answer, so it raises rather than inventing one."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(call-with-values (lambda () (hash-table-pop! (hash-table 'a 1))) list)" :note "(a 1) — the key and the value, as two values collected into a list.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(let ((h (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2)))\n  (let-values (((k v) (hash-table-pop! h)))\n    (list 'popped k v 'left (hash-table-keys h))))" :note "(popped a 1 left (b)) — the popped key is gone from the table.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(let ((work (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3)))\n  (let loop ((sum 0) (order (list)))\n    (if (hash-table-empty? work)\n        (begin (display (list 'sum sum 'order (reverse order))) (newline))\n        (let-values (((k v) (hash-table-pop! work)))\n          (loop (+ sum v) (cons k order))))))" :note "(sum 6 order (a b c)) — the drain loop: guard, pop, recur. The guard is not optional.")
  )
  :caveats (
    "Popping an EMPTY table raises: \"the table is empty, so there is nothing to pop\". Always guard with hash-table-empty? before calling, especially at the bottom of a loop."
    "It returns TWO values, not a pair. (define x (hash-table-pop! h)) does not give you a key-value pair. Use let-values, define-values, or call-with-values."
    "MOTOI BEHAVIOUR beyond SRFI-125: the spec says the removed association is ARBITRARY. Motoi always pops the OLDEST — the first key in insertion order — so the table behaves as a FIFO queue and drain order is reproducible. Portable code must not rely on this; Motoi examples may."
    "It MUTATES. The table shrinks by one on every call, which is what makes drain loops terminate — and what makes a pop inside a read-only-looking expression a bug."
    "There is no way to pop a SPECIFIC key. If you know the key, read it with hash-table-ref and remove it with hash-table-delete!."
  )
  :usecases ("Draining a work queue or task table until it is empty." "Sampling one entry out of a table when any entry will do." "Consuming a pending set in a worklist algorithm, where each item is processed exactly once and then discarded.")
  :related ("hash-table-empty?" "hash-table-delete!" "hash-table-find" "hash-table-clear!")
  :learn (:concept "Destructive take-one, and multiple return values as a way to hand back two things without allocating a container." :prerequisites ("hash-table-empty?" "hash-table-delete!" "let-values") :progression "First learn let-values on a simple two-value producer; then hash-table-pop! guarded by hash-table-empty?; then the drain loop, which is the reason the verb exists.")
  :provenance "SRFI-125 §The whole hash table (pop order is arbitrary in the spec; Motoi pops oldest-first — see :caveats); wired lib/std/srfi-125.js 2026-08-02; reference record 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define jobs (hash-table 'render 'pending 'save 'pending 'upload 'pending))\n(let loop ((done (list)))\n  (if (hash-table-empty? jobs)\n      (begin (display (list 'completed (reverse done))) (newline))\n      (let-values (((k v) (hash-table-pop! jobs)))\n        (display (list 'running k v))\n        (newline)\n        (loop (cons k done)))))"
     :math-program "(define h (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3))\n(define size-before (hash-table-size h))\n(let-values (((k v) (hash-table-pop! h)))\n  (display (list 'removed-one (= (hash-table-size h) (- size-before 1))\n                 'key-gone (not (hash-table-contains? h k))\n                 'value-was-its-image (= v 1)\n                 'domain-strictly-smaller (< (hash-table-size h) size-before))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define h (hash-table 'first 1 'second 2 'third 3))\n(define order (list))\n(let loop ()\n  (if (not (hash-table-empty? h))\n      (let-values (((k v) (hash-table-pop! h)))\n        (set! order (cons k order))\n        (loop))))\n(display (list 'pop-order (reverse order) 'fifo (equal? (reverse order) (list 'first 'second 'third))))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator is draining a job table: each job must run exactly once and then be forgotten, and the loop must stop when nothing is left. Three routes exist. Snapshotting hash-table-keys and iterating the list with for-each works, but the snapshot goes stale — a job added by another job during the run is never visited, and a job removed by another job is visited anyway, so the auditor has to prove no job touches the table. Reading the first key, then hash-table-ref, then hash-table-delete! works but is three calls that must agree about which key they mean, and between the read and the delete the table can change. hash-table-pop! removes and returns in one step, so the association the loop is holding is provably no longer in the table and cannot be handed out twice. The auditor's question 'can a job run twice?' is answered by the verb, not by an argument about the loop."
     :program "(define jobs (hash-table 'render 'pending 'save 'pending 'upload 'pending))\n(let loop ((done (list)))\n  (if (hash-table-empty? jobs)\n      (begin (display (reverse done)) (newline))\n      (let-values (((k v) (hash-table-pop! jobs)))\n        (loop (cons k done)))))\n(display (hash-table-size jobs))\n(newline)"
     :explanation "Line 1 builds the job table with three pending jobs. Line 2 opens the loop with an accumulator of finished names. Line 3 tests emptiness BEFORE popping, which is the precondition check; a bottom-tested loop would pop once too often and raise. Line 4 is the base case, printing the completed jobs in the order they ran. Lines 5-6 pop one association, binding the key and the value at once with let-values, and recur. Lines 7-8 print the final size, 0, which is the audit evidence that the loop consumed the whole table rather than exiting early. The tape shows exactly three pops for three jobs — the take-and-remove fusion is why that count cannot be four."
     :meta "Why hash-table-pop! and not a key snapshot: the snapshot is a copy of a fact that the loop then invalidates on every iteration, so its correctness depends on the body never touching the table — an obligation on code the auditor may not be able to see. Why not ref-then-delete: two calls with a gap between them, and the gap is where a re-entrant call can read the same entry again. Popping removes the gap.")
    (:eng "Guard at the top of the loop, and check the size after it to prove the drain was complete. The two together turn a loop that merely works today into one whose termination and completeness are both visible in the output."
     :math "The operation picks an element (k, v) of the graph of f and returns the pair together with the restricted function f|dom(f)\\{k}. Because the domain strictly shrinks with every call, iteration is well-founded and any drain loop terminates in exactly |dom(f)| steps."
     :cs "This is destructive take-any, the core primitive of worklist algorithms — Python's dict.popitem, Java's Iterator.next followed by remove. Motoi's oldest-first choice makes the worklist a FIFO queue, which gives breadth-first traversal order; an implementation that popped newest-first would give depth-first, from identical code.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Take one out and keep it: reach into a collection, remove some member, and come away holding both what identified it and what it was — so the collection is smaller by exactly one and you have everything you need to deal with what you took."
     :rungs ((:domain "hash" :code "(define jobs (hash-table 'render 'pending 'save 'pending))\n(let-values (((k v) (hash-table-pop! jobs)))\n  (display (list 'took k v 'left (hash-table-keys jobs))))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "taking the next ticket from a spike" :code "(define spike (hash-table 'ticket-1 'table-4 'ticket-2 'table-9 'ticket-3 'table-1))\n(let loop ((served (list)))\n  (if (hash-table-empty? spike)\n      (begin (display (list 'served (reverse served))) (newline))\n      (let-values (((ticket table) (hash-table-pop! spike)))\n        (loop (cons (list ticket 'to table) served)))))")
             (:domain "restriction of a function by removing one point" :code "(define f (hash-table 1 'a 2 'b 3 'c))\n(define before (hash-table-size f))\n(let-values (((k v) (hash-table-pop! f)))\n  (display (list 'point-removed k\n                 'its-image v\n                 'domain-shrank-by-one (= (hash-table-size f) (- before 1))\n                 'rest-unchanged (equal? (hash-table-keys f) (list 2 3)))))\n(newline)"))
     :explanation "In the hash-table domain the call removes one association and returns both halves. At a diner, the cook takes the next ticket off the spike: the ticket leaves the spike as it is read, so no order can be cooked twice, and the ticket carries both its number and the table it belongs to — one grab yields everything needed. In the function domain, removing a point from a partial function's domain gives the restriction f|D\\{k}, and the value f(k) comes back as the price of the restriction; the remaining function agrees with the original everywhere it is still defined. All three share the shape: a removal that yields what was removed, shrinking the source by exactly one."
     :meta "The shape transfers to every consume-once channel: a queue's dequeue, a stack's pop, a mailbox's receive, a scheduler taking the next runnable task. The defining property is the FUSION of read and remove — a design where they are separate operations admits the double-serve bug, which is why concurrent queues expose only the fused form."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define h (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3))\n(define before (hash-table-size h))\n(let-values (((k v) (hash-table-pop! h)))\n  (display (list 'key k 'value v\n                 'size-dropped-one (= (hash-table-size h) (- before 1))\n                 'key-removed (not (hash-table-contains? h k)))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (pop-conserves? kvs)\n  (let* ((h (apply hash-table kvs))\n         (before-size (hash-table-size h))\n         (before-keys (hash-table-keys h)))\n    (let-values (((k v) (hash-table-pop! h)))\n      (and (= (hash-table-size h) (- before-size 1))\n           (not (hash-table-contains? h k))\n           (member k before-keys)\n           (equal? (hash-table-keys h) (cdr before-keys))))))\n(display (list 'three (pop-conserves? (list 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3))\n               'one (pop-conserves? (list 'solo 9))\n               'two (pop-conserves? (list 'x 1 'y 2))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (drain-order kvs)\n  (let ((h (apply hash-table kvs)))\n    (let loop ((acc (list)))\n      (if (hash-table-empty? h)\n          (reverse acc)\n          (let-values (((k v) (hash-table-pop! h)))\n            (loop (cons k acc)))))))\n(define ks (list 'p 1 'q 2 'r 3 's 4))\n(display (list 'run-1 (drain-order ks)\n               'run-2 (drain-order ks)\n               'reproducible (equal? (drain-order ks) (drain-order ks))\n               'fifo (equal? (drain-order ks) (list 'p 'q 'r 's))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "Each hash-table-pop! removes exactly one association, returns its key and value as two values, leaves the key absent, and reduces the size by exactly one — so a guarded drain loop terminates after exactly size(h) iterations and visits every association once. In Motoi the order is oldest-first and reproducible."
     :program "(define h (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3 'd 4))\n(define start (hash-table-size h))\n(let loop ((n 0) (keys (list)) (sum 0))\n  (if (hash-table-empty? h)\n      (begin\n        (display (list 'pops n 'started-with start 'exactly-size (= n start)))\n        (newline)\n        (display (list 'keys (reverse keys) 'value-sum sum 'table-empty (hash-table-empty? h)))\n        (newline))\n      (let-values (((k v) (hash-table-pop! h)))\n        (loop (+ n 1) (cons k keys) (+ sum v)))))"
     :invariant "∀ non-empty h. pop!(h) = (k, v) with k ∈ dom(h) ∧ v = h(k) ∧ after: size(h') = size(h) - 1 ∧ k ∉ dom(h') ∧ ∀ j ≠ k. h'(j) = h(j) ⇒ a guarded drain loop runs exactly size(h) times and yields each association once"
     :meta "The pattern is strictly-decreasing-consumption, and it is the termination argument for every worklist algorithm: because the size is a non-negative integer that strictly decreases on every iteration and the guard recognises zero, the loop is well-founded. The claim has a completeness half too — the sum of the popped values equals the sum the table started with, which is what shows nothing was skipped or double-counted, and it is checked here rather than only counting iterations. What would falsify: a size that dropped by two (a double removal), a popped key still present, a drain loop running more or fewer times than the starting size, or a value sum that does not match. See book-of-code ch10 on worklist termination and book-of-math ch05 on well-founded orders."
     :eng "The engineering payoff is a termination proof you get for free. Any loop whose body pops and whose guard is emptiness cannot run away, no matter what else the body does — provided the body does not add more than it removes."
     :math "The claim is that pop is a restriction operator paired with an evaluation: it returns (k, f(k)) and the restricted function f|dom(f)\\{k}. The strict decrease of |dom(f)| under a well-founded order on the naturals is exactly the induction principle that licenses the drain loop."
     :cs "This is the consume-once contract for a destructive iterator. The reproducibility clause is Motoi-specific and follows from the insertion-ordered backing store; under a genuinely unordered implementation the count and completeness claims would still hold while the ORDER claim would not.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define queue (hash-table 'a 'load 'b 'parse))\n(define log (list))\n(let loop ()\n  (if (not (hash-table-empty? queue))\n      (let-values (((k v) (hash-table-pop! queue)))\n        (set! log (cons (list 'did k v) log))\n        (if (eq? v 'load) (hash-table-set! queue (list k 'child) 'parse))\n        (loop))))\n(display (list 'log (reverse log) 'drained (hash-table-empty? queue)))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define graph (hash-table 'a (list 'b 'c) 'b (list 'd) 'c (list 'd) 'd (list)))\n(define (reachable-from start)\n  (let ((frontier (hash-table start #t))\n        (seen (make-hash-table)))\n    (let loop ((order (list)))\n      (if (hash-table-empty? frontier)\n          (reverse order)\n          (let-values (((node _) (hash-table-pop! frontier)))\n            (if (hash-table-contains? seen node)\n                (loop order)\n                (begin\n                  (hash-table-set! seen node #t)\n                  (for-each (lambda (n) (if (not (hash-table-contains? seen n)) (hash-table-set! frontier n #t)))\n                            (hash-table-ref/default graph node (list)))\n                  (loop (cons node order)))))))))\n(display (reachable-from 'a))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define pending (hash-table 'j1 3 'j2 1 'j3 2))\n(define (round-robin! q budget)\n  (let loop ((ticks 0) (trace (list)))\n    (cond ((hash-table-empty? q) (list 'finished-at ticks 'trace (reverse trace)))\n          ((> ticks budget) (list 'budget-exhausted 'left (hash-table-size q) 'trace (reverse trace)))\n          (else\n            (let-values (((k v) (hash-table-pop! q)))\n              (if (> v 1) (hash-table-set! q k (- v 1)))\n              (loop (+ ticks 1) (cons k trace)))))))\n(display (round-robin! pending 20))\n(newline)"
     :base "hash-table-pop! removes one association and returns its key and value as two values."
     :composition "(define graph (hash-table 'a (list 'b 'c) 'b (list 'd) 'c (list 'd) 'd (list)))\n(define (reachable-from start)\n  (let ((frontier (hash-table start #t))\n        (seen (make-hash-table)))\n    (let loop ((order (list)))\n      (if (hash-table-empty? frontier)\n          (reverse order)\n          (let-values (((node _) (hash-table-pop! frontier)))\n            (if (hash-table-contains? seen node)\n                (loop order)\n                (begin\n                  (hash-table-set! seen node #t)\n                  (for-each (lambda (n) (hash-table-set! frontier n #t))\n                            (hash-table-ref/default graph node (list)))\n                  (loop (cons node order)))))))))\n(display (reachable-from 'a))\n(newline)"
     :emergent "graph traversal — a frontier table popped until empty, with a seen table preventing revisits, is exactly breadth-first search; the pop order decides the traversal strategy and nothing else in the code does"
     :meta "The composition chains hash-table-pop! (take the next node), hash-table-contains? (have I been here), and hash-table-set! (push the neighbours). What emerges is the traversal family, and the striking part is how little of the algorithm is written down: there is no queue type, no visited list, no explicit BFS. The strategy lives entirely in which association pop chooses — because Motoi pops oldest-first the frontier behaves as a FIFO queue and the traversal is breadth-first, and an implementation that popped newest-first would make the identical source code depth-first. That is the lesson worth keeping: in worklist algorithms the container's removal policy IS the algorithm. Termination comes free from the strictly-decreasing frontier plus the seen guard, which together bound the total pops by the node count. The same family appears as a scheduler's run queue, as a garbage collector's mark stack, as a build system's ready set, as a solver's agenda. See book-of-composition ch10 on worklist algorithms.")
)
)

(verb :name "hash-table-prune!" :std "SRFI-125" :library "hash" :tier "core" :kind "mut" :signature "(hash-table-prune! proc h) -> void"
  :summary "REMOVE every association for which (proc key value) is true. Mutates h in place; returns nothing useful. Procedure first. SRFI-125."
  :explanation "The destructive filter — with the polarity REVERSED from what most people expect. filter keeps what matches; prune! DELETES what matches. Reading it as 'prune away the ones that satisfy this' is the way to keep it straight. The procedure receives both the key and the value, so the condition can be about either. Because it mutates, the table shrinks under any other reference to it; take a hash-table-copy first if you need the original."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(let ((h (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3)))\n  (hash-table-prune! (lambda (k v) (odd? v)) h)\n  (hash-table-keys h))" :note "(b) — the odd-valued entries were REMOVED, not kept. Prune deletes what matches.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(let ((cache (hash-table 'a 'fresh 'b 'stale 'c 'stale 'd 'fresh)))\n  (hash-table-prune! (lambda (k v) (eq? v 'stale)) cache)\n  (list (hash-table-keys cache) (hash-table-size cache)))" :note "((a d) 2) — evicting the stale entries is a one-line prune.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(let* ((base (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3 'd 4))\n       (evens (hash-table-copy base)))\n  (hash-table-prune! (lambda (k v) (odd? v)) evens)\n  (display (list 'kept (hash-table-values evens)\n                 'base-intact (hash-table-values base)))\n  (newline))" :note "(kept (2 4) base-intact (1 2 3 4)) — copy-then-prune is how you get a non-destructive filter, since Motoi has no hash-table-filter.")
  )
  :caveats (
    "POLARITY. It deletes the matches; it does not keep them. To KEEP the entries satisfying p, prune with the negation: (hash-table-prune! (lambda (k v) (not (p k v))) h). Getting this backwards produces exactly the complement of the intended table, which is the single most common bug with this verb."
    "The argument order is PROCEDURE FIRST, table second — unlike hash-table-count and hash-table-intern!."
    "It MUTATES in place and returns nothing useful. (define kept (hash-table-prune! p h)) binds an unspecified value, not a table."
    "Truthiness is Scheme truthiness: only #f spares an entry. A procedure returning 0 or the empty list DELETES, because those are true values."
    "There is no non-destructive sibling in Motoi — SRFI-125 has no hash-table-filter either. Use hash-table-copy first when the original must survive."
    "The implementation collects the doomed keys before deleting any of them, so the procedure sees the table as it was at entry. Mutating the table from inside the procedure is still a bad idea, but it will not corrupt the traversal."
    "Pruning everything is legal and leaves an empty table; pruning nothing is legal and leaves it unchanged."
  )
  :usecases ("Cache eviction — drop every entry marked stale or expired." "Garbage collection of a registry — remove entries whose owner is gone." "Narrowing a working table to a sub-population before an expensive pass.")
  :related ("hash-table-delete!" "hash-table-count" "hash-table-copy" "hash-table-clear!")
  :learn (:concept "Destructive filtering, and the discipline of reading a predicate's polarity from the verb rather than from habit." :prerequisites ("hash-table-delete!" "lambda" "not") :progression "Write the delete-by-key loop yourself first so the two-phase collect-then-delete structure is visible; then use hash-table-prune!; then learn copy-then-prune as the non-destructive form.")
  :provenance "SRFI-125 §The whole hash table; wired lib/std/srfi-125.js 2026-08-02; reference record 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define cache (hash-table 'a 'fresh 'b 'stale 'c 'stale 'd 'fresh 'e 'stale))\n(define before (hash-table-size cache))\n(hash-table-prune! (lambda (k v) (eq? v 'stale)) cache)\n(display (list 'before before 'after (hash-table-size cache) 'kept (hash-table-keys cache)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define f (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3 'd 4 'e 5))\n(define p (lambda (k v) (even? v)))\n(define matching (hash-table-count f p))\n(define total (hash-table-size f))\n(hash-table-prune! p f)\n(display (list 'removed matching\n               'remaining (hash-table-size f)\n               'complement-law (= (hash-table-size f) (- total matching))\n               'nothing-matching-left (= 0 (hash-table-count f p))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define h (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3 'd 4))\n(define keep-p (lambda (k v) (> v 2)))\n(hash-table-prune! (lambda (k v) (not (keep-p k v))) h)\n(display (list 'kept-what-we-wanted (hash-table-keys h)\n               'all-survivors-satisfy (= (hash-table-size h) (hash-table-count h keep-p))))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator has to evict stale cache entries at the end of a frame. The condition is known, the entries are identified only by that condition, and the cache must shrink in place because other code holds the same reference. Three routes exist. Walking with hash-table-for-each and calling hash-table-delete! from inside the walk works in Motoi — because the traversal runs over a snapshot — but it depends on that snapshot behaviour, which SRFI-125 does not promise, so the code would break on a conforming implementation that iterates live. Collecting the doomed keys into a list and then deleting them in a second pass is portable and correct, but it is five lines and an intermediate list, and the auditor has to check that the two phases agree about the predicate. hash-table-prune! is that two-phase algorithm with the phases fused and the intermediate list hidden. The auditor's question 'what survives?' is answered by negating one predicate."
     :program "(define cache (hash-table 'a 'fresh 'b 'stale 'c 'stale 'd 'fresh))\n(display (list 'before (hash-table-keys cache)))\n(newline)\n(hash-table-prune! (lambda (k v) (eq? v 'stale)) cache)\n(display (list 'after (hash-table-keys cache)))\n(newline)"
     :explanation "Line 1 builds the cache with two fresh and two stale entries. Lines 2-3 print the keys before the eviction so the audit tape has a baseline — without it the reader cannot tell whether the prune removed anything. Line 4 prunes: the predicate returns true for the stale entries, and true means DELETE. Lines 5-6 print the surviving keys, (a d). The tape shows four keys in and two out, and the two that left are exactly the two the predicate selected. Note that the same reference, cache, is used before and after; the table was modified in place rather than replaced, which is what other holders of that reference will see."
     :meta "Why hash-table-prune! and not a walk with delete: the walk-and-delete form relies on the traversal tolerating mutation, which SRFI-125 does not guarantee, so the code is correct only by accident of implementation. Why not collect-then-delete by hand: it is the right algorithm, and prune! IS it — writing it out adds an intermediate list and a second place for the predicate to be typed differently.")
    (:eng "Print the size before and after. A prune with a predicate that never matches and a prune that was never reached look identical from the outside, and the pair of sizes is the cheapest way to tell them apart."
     :math "Given a predicate P over the graph of f, the operation replaces f by its restriction to the complement of P: f' = f|{k : ¬P(k, f(k))}. The remaining table satisfies count(f', P) = 0, and its size is exactly size(f) − count(f, P), so pruning is subtraction on cardinalities."
     :cs "This is remove-if / retain-if-not: Java's Collection.removeIf, Python's del-in-a-loop-over-a-key-snapshot, C++'s erase-remove idiom. The two-phase structure — decide, then delete — is universal, because deleting during a traversal invalidates iterators in most implementations.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Weeding: go through a collection, judge each member against a condition, and take out the ones that meet it — the collection itself is what changes, and what you are left with is defined by the negation of the judgement."
     :rungs ((:domain "hash" :code "(define cache (hash-table 'a 'fresh 'b 'stale 'c 'stale))\n(hash-table-prune! (lambda (k v) (eq? v 'stale)) cache)\n(display (hash-table-keys cache))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "pulling weeds from a bed" :code "(define bed (hash-table 'row-1 'carrot 'row-2 'thistle 'row-3 'carrot 'row-4 'nettle))\n(define planted (hash-table-size bed))\n(hash-table-prune! (lambda (row plant) (or (eq? plant 'thistle) (eq? plant 'nettle))) bed)\n(display (list 'was planted 'now (hash-table-size bed) 'crop (hash-table-values bed)))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "restricting a function to a subdomain" :code "(define f (hash-table 1 10 2 20 3 30 4 40))\n(define keep? (lambda (k v) (even? k)))\n(hash-table-prune! (lambda (k v) (not (keep? k v))) f)\n(display (list 'restricted-domain (hash-table-keys f)\n               'agrees-where-defined (equal? (hash-table-values f) (list 20 40))\n               'undefined-elsewhere (equal? 'UNDEF (hash-table-ref/default f 1 'UNDEF))))\n(newline)"))
     :explanation "In the hash-table domain the predicate names what goes, and what remains is everything else. A gardener weeding a bed pulls what is judged a weed; the bed is not copied to a new bed, it is the same bed with less in it, and the crop is defined negatively as what was not pulled. In the function domain, restricting f to a subdomain removes points from the domain — the surviving function agrees with the original wherever it is still defined and is undefined where the pruned points were, which the third rung checks by reading a removed key and getting the default back. All three share the shape: an in-place narrowing whose result is characterised by the complement of a condition."
     :meta "The shape transfers to every in-place narrowing: DELETE ... WHERE in SQL, a log rotation dropping old files, a garbage collector sweeping unmarked objects, a firewall dropping matching packets. Wherever it appears, the same trap appears with it — the condition names what LEAVES, and the thing you care about is what stays, so the specification and the code are always in opposite polarity."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define h (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3 'd 4))\n(define before (hash-table-size h))\n(define doomed (hash-table-count h (lambda (k v) (odd? v))))\n(hash-table-prune! (lambda (k v) (odd? v)) h)\n(display (list 'before before 'doomed doomed 'after (hash-table-size h)\n               'arithmetic (= (hash-table-size h) (- before doomed))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (prune-law kvs p)\n  (let* ((h (apply hash-table kvs))\n         (total (hash-table-size h))\n         (matching (hash-table-count h p)))\n    (hash-table-prune! p h)\n    (and (= (hash-table-size h) (- total matching))\n         (= 0 (hash-table-count h p))\n         (= (hash-table-size h) (hash-table-count h (lambda (k v) (not (p k v))))))))\n(define kvs (list 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3 'd 4 'e 5))\n(display (list 'odd (prune-law kvs (lambda (k v) (odd? v)))\n               'all (prune-law kvs (lambda (k v) #t))\n               'none (prune-law kvs (lambda (k v) #f))\n               'empty-table (prune-law (list) (lambda (k v) #t))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (survivors-unchanged? kvs p)\n  (let* ((h (apply hash-table kvs))\n         (snapshot (hash-table-copy h)))\n    (hash-table-prune! p h)\n    (let ((ok #t))\n      (hash-table-for-each\n        (lambda (k v)\n          (if (not (equal? v (hash-table-ref/default snapshot k 'MISS))) (set! ok #f)))\n        h)\n      ok)))\n(define kvs (list 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3 'd 4))\n(display (list 'odd (survivors-unchanged? kvs (lambda (k v) (odd? v)))\n               'big (survivors-unchanged? kvs (lambda (k v) (> v 2)))\n               'none (survivors-unchanged? kvs (lambda (k v) #f))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "hash-table-prune! removes exactly the matching associations and nothing else: the new size equals the old size minus the match count, no matching association remains, every survivor keeps the value it had, and the surviving count equals the count of the negated predicate."
     :program "(define h (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3 'd 4 'e 5))\n(define snapshot (hash-table-copy h))\n(define p (lambda (k v) (odd? v)))\n(define total (hash-table-size h))\n(define doomed (hash-table-count h p))\n(hash-table-prune! p h)\n(display (list 'total total 'doomed doomed 'left (hash-table-size h)\n               'subtraction (= (hash-table-size h) (- total doomed))))\n(newline)\n(define survivors-intact #t)\n(hash-table-for-each\n  (lambda (k v) (if (not (equal? v (hash-table-ref/default snapshot k 'MISS))) (set! survivors-intact #f)))\n  h)\n(display (list 'none-matching-left (= 0 (hash-table-count h p))\n               'survivors-unmodified survivors-intact\n               'complement-count (= (hash-table-size h) (hash-table-count snapshot (lambda (k v) (not (p k v)))))))\n(newline)"
     :invariant "∀ h P. after prune!(P, h): size(h') = size(h) − count(h, P) ∧ count(h', P) = 0 ∧ ∀ k ∈ dom(h'). h'(k) = h(k) ∧ dom(h') = dom(h) \\ {k : P(k, h(k))}"
     :meta "The pattern is exact-complement-removal, and it needs three checks because a prune can fail in three independent ways. Counting alone (the subtraction law) catches removing too few or too many but not removing the WRONG ones — a prune that deleted the complement would satisfy the arithmetic on a balanced table and be maximally wrong. The zero-remaining check catches under-removal directly. The survivors-unmodified check, which requires a copy taken beforehand, catches an implementation that rebuilds the table and loses or alters values in the process. Together they pin the result exactly. What would falsify: a size that does not match the subtraction, a matching entry still present, or a survivor whose value changed. See book-of-code ch09 on in-place modification contracts and book-of-math ch03 on set complements."
     :eng "The engineering payoff is that a prune can be asserted in one line — count the predicate before, compare sizes after — which turns a whole class of polarity bugs into a failing test rather than a slowly-emptying cache."
     :math "The claim is that prune implements the restriction f|D\\S where S = {k : P(k, f(k))}. Restriction is exactly characterised by two conditions — the domain is the set difference, and the function agrees with the original on the surviving domain — and both are checked."
     :cs "This is the remove-if contract. Note that the implementation collects the doomed keys before deleting any, which is what makes the predicate see a consistent snapshot; an implementation that deleted during the scan would have undefined behaviour under most iterator contracts.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define registry (hash-table 'e1 'alive 'e2 'dead 'e3 'alive 'e4 'dead 'e5 'dead))\n(define (sweep! r)\n  (let ((before (hash-table-size r)))\n    (hash-table-prune! (lambda (k v) (eq? v 'dead)) r)\n    (list 'collected (- before (hash-table-size r)) 'live (hash-table-size r))))\n(display (sweep! registry))\n(newline)\n(display (sweep! registry))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (sieve-primes limit)\n  (let ((candidates (make-hash-table)))\n    (for-each (lambda (n) (if (>= n 2) (hash-table-set! candidates n #t))) (iota (+ limit 1)))\n    (for-each\n      (lambda (d)\n        (if (hash-table-contains? candidates d)\n            (hash-table-prune! (lambda (n _) (and (> n d) (= 0 (modulo n d)))) candidates)))\n      (iota (+ limit 1)))\n    (sort (hash-table-keys candidates) <)))\n(display (sieve-primes 40))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define store (hash-table 'k1 5 'k2 1 'k3 9 'k4 2 'k5 7))\n(define (age-out! s ttl)\n  (hash-table-for-each (lambda (k v) (hash-table-set! s k (- v 1))) s)\n  (hash-table-prune! (lambda (k v) (<= v 0)) s)\n  (hash-table-size s))\n(let loop ((tick 0) (sizes (list)))\n  (if (> tick 5)\n      (begin (display (list 'sizes-over-time (reverse sizes))) (newline))\n      (loop (+ tick 1) (cons (age-out! store 0) sizes))))"
     :base "hash-table-prune! removes in place every association satisfying a two-argument predicate."
     :composition "(define registry (hash-table 'e1 'alive 'e2 'dead 'e3 'alive 'e4 'dead))\n(define (sweep! r)\n  (let ((before (hash-table-size r)))\n    (hash-table-prune! (lambda (k v) (eq? v 'dead)) r)\n    (list 'collected (- before (hash-table-size r)) 'live (hash-table-size r))))\n(display (sweep! registry))\n(newline)\n(display (sweep! registry))\n(newline)"
     :emergent "the sweep phase of mark-and-sweep — a repeated prune whose second run is a no-op, so the operation is idempotent and safe to schedule on a timer without coordinating with anything else"
     :meta "The composition chains hash-table-size (measure), hash-table-prune! (remove the dead), and hash-table-size again (measure), and then runs the whole thing TWICE. The second run collecting zero is the point: because the predicate selects a set that the prune empties, applying it again changes nothing, which makes the sweep idempotent. Idempotence is what lets a garbage collector be triggered by a timer, by memory pressure, and by an explicit call, all without coordination — extra runs are free rather than harmful. The math composition pushes the same primitive further: a sieve is nothing but repeated pruning by a family of predicates, and it terminates because each prune shrinks the candidate set or leaves it alone. The same family appears as generational GC's sweep, as log rotation, as a cache's TTL reaper, as SQL's periodic DELETE of expired rows. See book-of-composition ch11 on idempotent maintenance operations.")
)
)

(verb :name "hash-table-update!/default" :std "SRFI-125" :library "hash" :tier "core" :kind "mut" :signature "(hash-table-update!/default h k proc default) -> void"
  :summary "Set h[k] to (proc current), where current is the existing value or, if k is unbound, the plain default VALUE. Table first. SRFI-125."
  :explanation "The accumulate-into-a-slot verb, and the one that makes counting and grouping short. Where hash-table-update! wants a thunk for the missing case, this one wants a plain value, which is what you want when the fallback is a cheap constant like 0 or the empty list. The key does not have to exist — the first update creates it — so there is no seeding pass and no is-this-the-first-time branch. Note that the default is evaluated eagerly, like any argument, so it must be cheap and effect-free."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(let ((h (make-hash-table)))\n  (hash-table-update!/default h 'n (lambda (v) (+ v 1)) 0)\n  (hash-table-ref/default h 'n 'missing))" :note "1 — the key did not exist, so proc was applied to the default 0.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(let ((tally (make-hash-table)))\n  (for-each (lambda (w) (hash-table-update!/default tally w (lambda (n) (+ n 1)) 0))\n            (list 'a 'b 'a 'c 'a 'b))\n  (list (hash-table-keys tally) (hash-table-values tally)))" :note "((a b c) (3 2 1)) — the whole word-count idiom, with no seeding pass.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(let ((groups (make-hash-table)))\n  (for-each (lambda (p)\n              (hash-table-update!/default groups (car p) (lambda (xs) (cons (cadr p) xs)) (list)))\n            (list (list 'fruit 'apple) (list 'tool 'saw) (list 'fruit 'pear)))\n  (display (list (hash-table-keys groups)\n                 (map reverse (hash-table-values groups))))\n  (newline))" :note "((fruit tool) ((apple pear) (saw))) — with the empty list as the default, the same verb groups instead of counts.")
  )
  :caveats (
    "The fourth argument is a plain VALUE, not a thunk. It is evaluated eagerly on every call, hit or miss, so an expensive or side-effecting default is paid even when the key is already present — use hash-table-update! with a failure thunk in that case."
    "proc receives ONE argument, the current-or-default value. It does not receive the key. If proc needs the key, close over it or use hash-table-set! with an explicit read."
    "The argument order is TABLE FIRST — table, key, procedure, default. Its search-shaped siblings hash-table-find, hash-table-map and hash-table-prune! take the procedure first."
    "It returns nothing useful. To see the new value, read it back with hash-table-ref/default."
    "The default is shared, not copied. Using a MUTABLE default such as a pre-built vector means every fresh key starts out pointing at the same object; with the empty list this is safe because cons allocates."
    "The key is created on the first call even if proc returns the default unchanged, so a no-op update still grows the table."
  )
  :usecases ("Counting occurrences — the tally or word-count idiom." "Grouping — accumulating a list per key with the empty list as the default." "Running totals, min/max tracking, and any per-key accumulator where the starting value is a constant.")
  :related ("hash-table-update!" "hash-table-intern!" "hash-table-ref/default" "hash-table-set!")
  :learn (:concept "Read-modify-write in one step, with a constant supplied for the first time through." :prerequisites ("hash-table-ref/default" "hash-table-set!" "lambda") :progression "Write ref/default-then-set! by hand first so the read-modify-write shape is visible; then collapse it to hash-table-update!/default; then learn hash-table-update! for when the missing-key case needs real work, and hash-table-intern! for when you want to keep the first value rather than transform it.")
  :provenance "SRFI-125 §The whole hash table; wired lib/std/srfi-125.js 2026-07-24; reference record 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define damage-taken (make-hash-table))\n(define (hit! who amount)\n  (hash-table-update!/default damage-taken who (lambda (total) (+ total amount)) 0))\n(hit! 'hero 5)\n(hit! 'bat 2)\n(hit! 'hero 7)\n(display (list 'hero (hash-table-ref/default damage-taken 'hero 0)\n               'bat (hash-table-ref/default damage-taken 'bat 0)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define acc (make-hash-table))\n(define (add! k n) (hash-table-update!/default acc k (lambda (v) (+ v n)) 0))\n(add! 'x 3) (add! 'x 4) (add! 'y 10) (add! 'x 0)\n(display (list 'x (hash-table-ref/default acc 'x 'MISS)\n               'y (hash-table-ref/default acc 'y 'MISS)\n               'identity-element-worked (= 7 (hash-table-ref/default acc 'x 0))\n               'fresh-key-starts-at-zero (= 10 (hash-table-ref/default acc 'y 0))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define by-hand (make-hash-table))\n(define fused (make-hash-table))\n(define events (list 'a 'b 'a 'c 'b 'a))\n(for-each (lambda (e) (hash-table-set! by-hand e (+ 1 (hash-table-ref/default by-hand e 0)))) events)\n(for-each (lambda (e) (hash-table-update!/default fused e (lambda (n) (+ n 1)) 0)) events)\n(display (list 'by-hand (hash-table-values by-hand)\n               'fused (hash-table-values fused)\n               'identical (and (equal? (hash-table-keys by-hand) (hash-table-keys fused))\n                               (equal? (hash-table-values by-hand) (hash-table-values fused)))))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator is totalling damage per entity as combat events stream in. Entities appear for the first time mid-fight, so no seeding pass is possible, and the first hit on an unseen entity must start from zero rather than raise. Three routes exist. Reading with hash-table-ref/default and writing back with hash-table-set! works, but it names the key twice and the default's meaning is split across two calls, so a typo in the second key silently creates a parallel entry. hash-table-update! works but demands a THUNK for the missing case, which wraps a bare 0 in a lambda that exists only to satisfy the signature. hash-table-intern! is the wrong verb entirely — it keeps the first value and would record only the first hit. hash-table-update!/default takes the constant directly and names the key once. The auditor reads one call and knows the key, the transformation and the starting point."
     :program "(define damage-taken (make-hash-table))\n(define (hit! who amount)\n  (hash-table-update!/default damage-taken who (lambda (total) (+ total amount)) 0))\n(hit! 'hero 5)\n(hit! 'bat 2)\n(hit! 'hero 7)\n(display (hash-table-ref/default damage-taken 'hero 0))\n(newline)\n(display (hash-table-ref/default damage-taken 'bat 0))\n(newline)"
     :explanation "Line 1 makes the empty tally — empty is correct, because the set of entities that will take damage is not known in advance. Lines 2-3 define the recording function; the lambda closes over amount and receives the running total, and the 0 is the value used the first time a name is seen. Lines 4-6 record three hits across two entities. Lines 7-8 print hero's total, 12, which is 5 + 7 — two updates to a key that did not exist before the first one. Lines 9-10 print bat's total, 2, from a single update. The audit tape shows no seeding step and no first-time branch, and that absence is the point: every hit! call is identical whether or not the entity has been seen."
     :meta "Why hash-table-update!/default and not ref/default-then-set!: the two-call form mentions the key twice and the default once, so the reader must check that both key expressions agree; the fused form mentions the key once and cannot disagree with itself. Why not hash-table-update!: its missing-key argument is a thunk, so a constant 0 has to be written (lambda () 0) — noise that says nothing. Why not intern!: intern keeps the first value forever, so the second hit would be lost.")
    (:eng "Give the accumulator a name in the lambda that says what it is — total, count, seen — not v. The whole readability win of this verb is that the read and the write are adjacent, and a well-named parameter is what carries the meaning across them."
     :math "The operation is the point-update f' = f[k ↦ g(f(k) if defined, else d)]. With g = (+ 1) and d = 0 the table becomes a counting measure over the key stream; with g = cons and d = () it becomes a fibre map, sending each key to the list of items that landed on it. The default is the identity element of whatever monoid the accumulation is using, which is why 0 goes with + and () goes with cons."
     :cs "This is the compute-if-absent-then-merge primitive: Java's Map.merge, Python's collections.defaultdict combined with an update, C++'s operator[] with value-initialisation. All of them exist because the read-modify-write triple is the single most common thing anyone does to a map, and splitting it invites a mismatch between the read's key and the write's.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Adding to a running total that might not exist yet: name a slot, say how to combine what is there with what has arrived, and say what to assume was there if the slot is new — so the first contribution and the hundredth are handled by the same code."
     :rungs ((:domain "hash" :code "(define tally (make-hash-table))\n(for-each (lambda (w) (hash-table-update!/default tally w (lambda (n) (+ n 1)) 0))\n          (list 'a 'b 'a 'c 'a))\n(display (list (hash-table-keys tally) (hash-table-values tally)))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "a tab that opens itself when the first drink is ordered" :code "(define tabs (make-hash-table))\n(define (order! who price)\n  (hash-table-update!/default tabs who (lambda (owed) (+ owed price)) 0))\n(order! 'ann 4)\n(order! 'bob 3)\n(order! 'ann 5)\n(display (list 'ann (hash-table-ref/default tabs 'ann 0)\n               'bob (hash-table-ref/default tabs 'bob 0)\n               'tabs-open (hash-table-size tabs)))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "folding a stream into the fibres of a map" :code "(define fibres (make-hash-table))\n(define (place! x)\n  (hash-table-update!/default fibres (modulo x 3) (lambda (xs) (cons x xs)) (list)))\n(for-each place! (list 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8))\n(display (list 'residues (sort (hash-table-keys fibres) <)\n               'fibre-of-0 (reverse (hash-table-ref/default fibres 0 (list)))\n               'partition-complete (= 8 (apply + (map length (hash-table-values fibres))))))\n(newline)"))
     :explanation "In the hash-table domain the update creates the key on first contact with 0 as the starting count. At a bar, a tab opens itself when the first drink is ordered — the barman does not check a list of open tabs, and every order is rung the same way whether it is the customer's first or fifth. In the math domain, sorting a stream into the fibres of a map means each element is consed onto the list for its image, and the empty list is the starting value for a fibre nobody has landed in yet; the check that the fibre lengths sum to the stream length is what shows the partition is complete. All three share the shape: a keyed accumulator with an implicit, constant starting value."
     :meta "The shape transfers to every accumulation over an open key set: a histogram, a per-user rate limiter, a per-file line counter, a per-tag index. The load-bearing element is the IDENTITY of the combining operation — 0 for addition, () for cons, the empty string for append, +infinity for min — and choosing a default that is not the identity is how these accumulators go subtly wrong."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define h (make-hash-table))\n(hash-table-update!/default h 'k (lambda (v) (+ v 1)) 0)\n(display (list 'after-first (hash-table-ref/default h 'k 'MISS) 'size (hash-table-size h)))\n(newline)\n(hash-table-update!/default h 'k (lambda (v) (+ v 1)) 0)\n(display (list 'after-second (hash-table-ref/default h 'k 'MISS) 'size (hash-table-size h)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (counts-match? xs)\n  (let ((tally (make-hash-table)))\n    (for-each (lambda (x) (hash-table-update!/default tally x (lambda (n) (+ n 1)) 0)) xs)\n    (and (= (length xs) (apply + (hash-table-values tally)))\n         (= (hash-table-size tally) (length (delete-duplicates xs)))\n         (equal? (hash-table-ref/default tally (car xs) 0)\n                 (length (filter (lambda (y) (equal? y (car xs))) xs))))))\n(display (list 'repeats (counts-match? (list 'a 'b 'a 'c 'a 'b))\n               'all-distinct (counts-match? (list 'p 'q 'r))\n               'all-same (counts-match? (list 'z 'z 'z 'z))\n               'singleton (counts-match? (list 'only))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (fused-equals-manual? xs)\n  (let ((fused (make-hash-table))\n        (manual (make-hash-table)))\n    (for-each (lambda (x) (hash-table-update!/default fused x (lambda (n) (+ n 1)) 0)) xs)\n    (for-each (lambda (x) (hash-table-set! manual x (+ 1 (hash-table-ref/default manual x 0)))) xs)\n    (and (equal? (hash-table-keys fused) (hash-table-keys manual))\n         (equal? (hash-table-values fused) (hash-table-values manual)))))\n(display (list 'mixed (fused-equals-manual? (list 'a 'b 'a 'c 'b 'a))\n               'empty (fused-equals-manual? (list))\n               'one (fused-equals-manual? (list 'solo))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "hash-table-update!/default applies proc to the existing value when the key is bound and to the default when it is not, always leaving the key bound afterwards; it is exactly equivalent to reading with hash-table-ref/default and writing back with hash-table-set!, so a tally built with it has values summing to the number of updates and a size equal to the number of distinct keys."
     :program "(define xs (list 'a 'b 'a 'c 'a 'b))\n(define fused (make-hash-table))\n(define manual (make-hash-table))\n(for-each (lambda (x) (hash-table-update!/default fused x (lambda (n) (+ n 1)) 0)) xs)\n(for-each (lambda (x) (hash-table-set! manual x (+ 1 (hash-table-ref/default manual x 0)))) xs)\n(display (list 'fused-keys (hash-table-keys fused) 'fused-values (hash-table-values fused)))\n(newline)\n(display (list 'equivalent-to-manual (and (equal? (hash-table-keys fused) (hash-table-keys manual))\n                                          (equal? (hash-table-values fused) (hash-table-values manual)))\n               'counts-sum-to-updates (= (length xs) (apply + (hash-table-values fused)))\n               'size-is-distinct-keys (= (hash-table-size fused) (length (delete-duplicates xs)))))\n(newline)"
     :invariant "∀ h k g d. after update!/default(h, k, g, d): ref(h, k) = g(ref(h, k) if k ∈ dom(h) else d) ∧ k ∈ dom(h') ∧ ∀ j ≠ k. h'(j) = h(j) ∧ the operation ≡ set!(h, k, g(ref/default(h, k, d)))"
     :meta "The pattern is fused-read-modify-write, and it is proved by EQUIVALENCE to the unfused form rather than by direct inspection — running both against the same input stream and comparing the whole tables is stronger than checking a few entries, because it compares key order too. The conservation law on top of that (the counts sum to the number of updates, the size equals the distinct-key count) is what catches an implementation that loses an update or creates a duplicate key, which the equivalence test alone would miss if both forms shared the bug. The empty-input case is included because an accumulator that mishandles the very first update is the classic failure. What would falsify: the fused and manual tables disagreeing, counts not summing to the update count, or a size larger than the distinct-key count. See book-of-code ch08 on read-modify-write and book-of-math ch04 on counting measures."
     :eng "The engineering payoff is that the fused form can be substituted for the hand-written pair anywhere without re-testing the logic, so an existing codebase can be tidied one call site at a time."
     :math "The claim is that the operation is point-update composed with totalised lookup: f[k ↦ g(f̄(k))] where f̄ is f totalised by the constant d. The counting law follows because with g = (+1) and d = 0 the resulting table is the counting measure of the input multiset, and a measure's total mass equals the number of elements."
     :cs "This is the merge / upsert-with-accumulator contract. Its practical value is atomicity of the read-modify-write triple: in a concurrent map the fused form can be made safe, while the unfused read-then-write cannot be without external locking.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (word-count words)\n  (let ((tally (make-hash-table)))\n    (for-each (lambda (w) (hash-table-update!/default tally w (lambda (n) (+ n 1)) 0)) words)\n    tally))\n(define wc (word-count (list 'the 'cat 'sat 'on 'the 'mat 'the 'end)))\n(hash-table-for-each (lambda (w n) (display (list w n)) (newline)) wc)\n(display (list 'distinct (hash-table-size wc) 'total (apply + (hash-table-values wc))))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (adjacency edges)\n  (let ((g (make-hash-table)))\n    (for-each (lambda (e)\n                (hash-table-update!/default g (car e) (lambda (ns) (cons (cadr e) ns)) (list))\n                (hash-table-update!/default g (cadr e) (lambda (ns) (cons (car e) ns)) (list)))\n              edges)\n    g))\n(define g (adjacency (list (list 'a 'b) (list 'b 'c) (list 'a 'c) (list 'c 'd))))\n(define (degree n) (length (hash-table-ref/default g n (list))))\n(display (list 'nodes (hash-table-keys g)\n               'degrees (map degree (hash-table-keys g))\n               'handshake-lemma (= (apply + (map degree (hash-table-keys g))) (* 2 4))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (bucket-sort-keys pairs slots)\n  (let ((buckets (make-hash-table)))\n    (for-each (lambda (p)\n                (hash-table-update!/default buckets (modulo (car p) slots)\n                                            (lambda (xs) (cons p xs)) (list)))\n              pairs)\n    (map (lambda (s) (list s (reverse (hash-table-ref/default buckets s (list)))))\n         (sort (hash-table-keys buckets) <))))\n(display (bucket-sort-keys (list (list 1 'a) (list 4 'b) (list 7 'c) (list 2 'd) (list 5 'e)) 3))\n(newline)"
     :base "hash-table-update!/default transforms the value at a key, using a constant default when the key is new."
     :composition "(define (word-count words)\n  (let ((tally (make-hash-table)))\n    (for-each (lambda (w) (hash-table-update!/default tally w (lambda (n) (+ n 1)) 0)) words)\n    tally))\n(define wc (word-count (list 'the 'cat 'sat 'on 'the 'mat 'the 'end)))\n(display (hash-table-keys wc))\n(newline)\n(display (hash-table-values wc))\n(newline)\n(display (apply + (hash-table-values wc)))\n(newline)"
     :emergent "the fold-into-a-map — a whole stream collapsed into a keyed summary in one pass, where the default supplies the monoid's identity and the procedure supplies its operation, so counting, grouping and adjacency-building are the SAME program with different arguments"
     :meta "The composition chains for-each over a stream with hash-table-update!/default into a shared accumulator. What emerges is the group-and-reduce family, and the striking part is how little changes between its members: counting uses (+1) with default 0, grouping uses cons with default (), adjacency-building uses cons with default () applied twice per edge, bucket sorting uses cons with a hashed key. Each pair (operation, default) is a MONOID, and the default must be that monoid's identity or the first contribution is wrong — that requirement is the whole design rule for this pattern. The handshake lemma in the math composition is the same self-audit idea seen elsewhere in this chapter: an independently known total that the accumulated structure must reproduce. The same family appears as MapReduce's reduce phase, as SQL's GROUP BY with an aggregate, as Python's Counter and defaultdict, as Spark's reduceByKey. See book-of-composition ch09 on group-and-reduce and book-of-math ch08 on monoids as accumulator specifications.")
)
)

(verb :name "hash-table-walk" :std "SRFI-125" :library "hash" :tier "core" :kind "mut" :signature "(hash-table-walk h proc) -> void"
  :summary "Call (proc key value) on every association for its side effect, TABLE first. The same operation as hash-table-for-each with the arguments swapped. SRFI-125."
  :explanation "The table-first spelling of traversal. It is the older name, inherited from SRFI-69 and carried forward, and it reads naturally when the table is the subject of the sentence — walk THIS TABLE doing THIS. Everything else about it is identical to hash-table-for-each: the procedure takes the key and the value, the return value is unspecified, and Motoi visits in insertion order. Motoi ships both spellings live rather than aliasing one to the other, so pick one per file; mixing them is how the argument-order mistake happens."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(hash-table-walk (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2) (lambda (k v) (display (list k v))))\n(newline)" :note "(a 1)(b 2) — same output as hash-table-for-each, arguments the other way round.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(let ((longest 'none))\n  (hash-table-walk (hash-table 'ox 2 'cat 3 'zebra 5)\n                   (lambda (k v) (if (eq? longest 'none) (set! longest k)\n                                     (if (> v (hash-table-ref/default (hash-table 'ox 2 'cat 3 'zebra 5) longest 0))\n                                         (set! longest k)))))\n  longest)" :note "zebra — a running maximum kept in a captured binding, because the walk itself returns nothing.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(let ((table (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3))\n      (from-walk (list))\n      (from-for-each (list)))\n  (hash-table-walk table (lambda (k v) (set! from-walk (cons (list k v) from-walk))))\n  (hash-table-for-each (lambda (k v) (set! from-for-each (cons (list k v) from-for-each))) table)\n  (display (list 'identical (equal? from-walk from-for-each)\n                 'order (map car (reverse from-walk))))\n  (newline))" :note "(identical #t order (a b c)) — the two spellings are the same operation, visiting in the same order.")
  )
  :caveats (
    "The argument order is TABLE FIRST — the exact reverse of hash-table-for-each. This is the whole difference between the two verbs, and it is the only thing that can go wrong when choosing between them."
    "SRFI-125 lists hash-table-walk among the names it DEPRECATES in favour of hash-table-for-each. Motoi ships both as live, unaliased bindings, so existing code keeps working; new code in a mixed codebase should prefer hash-table-for-each."
    "The return value is unspecified. Results must escape through a side effect — a captured binding, a display, or a write into another table."
    "MOTOI GUARANTEE beyond SRFI-125: iteration follows insertion order of each key's first binding; re-setting a key keeps its position, deleting and re-adding moves it to the end. SRFI-125 promises no order at all, so portable code must not depend on this."
    "Do not add or remove keys in the table being walked. The traversal runs over a snapshot taken at entry, so insertions are not visited and deletions still are — deterministic, but rarely intended."
    "A non-table argument is silently ignored rather than raising, so an argument-order slip can produce a walk that does nothing at all instead of an error."
  )
  :usecases ("Printing, logging or rendering every entry when the table reads as the subject of the sentence." "Porting SRFI-69 code, where hash-table-walk is the only spelling available." "Any effectful pass where the operator prefers the container-first reading.")
  :related ("hash-table-for-each" "hash-table-fold" "hash-table-map" "hash-table-keys")
  :learn (:concept "Two spellings of one operation, and the cost of argument-order variation in a standard library." :prerequisites ("hash-table-for-each") :progression "Learn hash-table-for-each first because it is the preferred spelling; learn hash-table-walk so you can read older code; then adopt one and stay with it.")
  :provenance "SRFI-125 §Mapping and folding — listed under the spec's deprecated names, kept live in Motoi; wired lib/std/srfi-125.js 2026-07-24; reference record 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define scores (hash-table 'ann 90 'bob 72 'cy 65))\n(hash-table-walk scores (lambda (name score) (display (list name 'scored score)) (newline)))\n(display (list 'rows (hash-table-size scores)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define f (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3))\n(define via-walk (list))\n(define via-for-each (list))\n(hash-table-walk f (lambda (k v) (set! via-walk (cons (list k v) via-walk))))\n(hash-table-for-each (lambda (k v) (set! via-for-each (cons (list k v) via-for-each))) f)\n(display (list 'same-enumeration (equal? via-walk via-for-each)\n               'covers-graph (= (length via-walk) (hash-table-size f))\n               'in-key-order (equal? (map car (reverse via-walk)) (hash-table-keys f))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define t (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3))\n(define ok #t)\n(hash-table-walk 5 (lambda (k v) (set! ok 'WALKED-A-NON-TABLE)))\n(display (list 'non-table-silently-ignored (eq? ok #t)))\n(newline)\n(define n 0)\n(hash-table-walk t (lambda (k v) (set! n (+ n 1))))\n(display (list 'real-table-visited n))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator is printing a score report and the table is the subject of the sentence — 'walk the scores, printing each'. Both traversal spellings do the identical job, so the choice is not about behaviour at all; it is about which reading the call site should have and about consistency with the rest of the file. hash-table-for-each reads procedure-first and matches R7RS-large and the list verb for-each, which is the argument for preferring it in new code. hash-table-walk reads table-first and matches the accessor verbs in this library — hash-table-size, hash-table-keys, hash-table-count — all of which take the table first, which is the argument for preferring it where the surrounding code is accessor-heavy. The auditor's real question here is not 'is this right?' but 'is this file consistent?', because a file that mixes both spellings will eventually get the arguments the wrong way round, and the failure is silent: a non-table argument is ignored rather than raising."
     :program "(define scores (hash-table 'ann 90 'bob 72 'cy 65))\n(hash-table-walk scores (lambda (name score) (display (list name 'scored score)) (newline)))\n(display (hash-table-size scores))\n(newline)"
     :explanation "Line 1 builds the score table. Line 2 walks it table-first, printing one line per entry; the lambda names its parameters name and score rather than k and v, which is what makes the call site readable at a glance. The three lines print in insertion order — ann, bob, cy — so the output is reproducible and can be diffed in a test. Lines 3-4 print the size, 3, matching the number of printed rows; that match is the audit evidence that the walk covered everything. The tape shows a read-only traversal: nothing in it can change the table, which is why the size afterwards is trustworthy as a count of what was visited."
     :meta "Why hash-table-walk and not hash-table-for-each: nothing about behaviour — they are the same operation. The choice is stylistic and should be made once per file. The genuine hazard is mixing them, because passing the arguments in the wrong order does not raise; the non-table argument is silently ignored and the walk does nothing, so the bug shows up as missing output rather than as an error.")
    (:eng "Choose one spelling per file and grep for the other before review. Because the wrong order fails silently, consistency is a real defect-prevention measure here rather than a matter of taste."
     :math "The traversal enumerates the graph of f, applying a procedure to each pair exactly once. The two spellings denote the same function of two arguments up to the order of those arguments — they are related by the flip combinator, and nothing else distinguishes them."
     :cs "This is the visitor pattern with a container-first calling convention. The SRFI's own deprecation of walk in favour of for-each is a lesson in library design: two names for one operation is a cost paid forever by every reader, which is why the spec chose one and why Motoi's keeping both is a compatibility decision rather than a design one.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "One operation, two spellings: the same exhaustive pass over a collection, written either subject-first or verb-first, with the choice carrying meaning for the reader and none for the machine."
     :rungs ((:domain "hash" :code "(define scores (hash-table 'ann 90 'bob 72))\n(hash-table-walk scores (lambda (name score) (display (list name score))))\n(newline)\n(hash-table-for-each (lambda (name score) (display (list name score))) scores)\n(newline)")
             (:domain "the same instruction given two ways" :code "(define shelf (hash-table 'top 'atlas 'middle 'novel 'bottom 'dictionary))\n(define dust! (lambda (where what) (display (list 'dusted what 'on-the where)) (newline)))\n(hash-table-walk shelf dust!)\n(display (list 'and-again 'proc-first))\n(newline)\n(hash-table-for-each dust! shelf)")
             (:domain "two applications related by flip" :code "(define f (hash-table 1 'a 2 'b))\n(define seen-1 (list))\n(define seen-2 (list))\n(hash-table-walk f (lambda (k v) (set! seen-1 (cons k seen-1))))\n(hash-table-for-each (lambda (k v) (set! seen-2 (cons k seen-2))) f)\n(display (list 'walk seen-1 'for-each seen-2 'flip-equivalent (equal? seen-1 seen-2)))\n(newline)"))
     :explanation "In the hash-table domain the two calls produce identical output from identically-shaped procedures with the arguments transposed. Giving the same instruction two ways — dust the shelf / for each book, dust it — changes which noun the listener holds in mind first and changes nothing about what gets dusted. In the combinator domain the relationship is exactly flip: walk = flip(for-each), and applying either to the same pair of arguments in the appropriate order yields the same effects in the same sequence, which the third rung verifies by comparing the two visit lists. All three share the shape: an operation whose meaning is invariant under a permutation of its arguments, so the permutation is free to carry stylistic information instead."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever a library offers method-style and function-style spellings of one operation: obj.map(f) against map(f, obj), a pipeline operator against nested calls, SQL's two join syntaxes. The recurring lesson is that the redundancy is never quite free — it costs every reader a moment deciding which one they are looking at, and it costs the writer a silent failure mode whenever the two orders are type-compatible, which here they are not, but only because a non-table argument happens to be ignored rather than raising."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define t (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3))\n(define n 0)\n(hash-table-walk t (lambda (k v) (set! n (+ n 1))))\n(display (list 'visits n 'size (hash-table-size t) 'exactly-once (= n (hash-table-size t))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (walk-equals-for-each? kvs)\n  (let ((t (apply hash-table kvs))\n        (a (list))\n        (b (list)))\n    (hash-table-walk t (lambda (k v) (set! a (cons (list k v) a))))\n    (hash-table-for-each (lambda (k v) (set! b (cons (list k v) b))) t)\n    (and (equal? a b)\n         (= (length a) (hash-table-size t))\n         (equal? (map car (reverse a)) (hash-table-keys t)))))\n(display (list 'three (walk-equals-for-each? (list 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3))\n               'one (walk-equals-for-each? (list 'solo 9))\n               'empty (walk-equals-for-each? (list))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define t (hash-table 'w 1 'x 2 'y 3 'z 4))\n(define (walk-order)\n  (let ((acc (list)))\n    (hash-table-walk t (lambda (k v) (set! acc (cons k acc))))\n    (reverse acc)))\n(display (list 'run-1 (walk-order) 'run-2 (walk-order)\n               'stable (equal? (walk-order) (walk-order))\n               'matches-keys (equal? (walk-order) (hash-table-keys t))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "hash-table-walk and hash-table-for-each are the same operation with transposed arguments: given the same table and procedure they visit the same associations, in the same order, exactly once each, and that order is hash-table-keys paired with hash-table-values."
     :program "(define t (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3))\n(define from-walk (list))\n(define from-for-each (list))\n(hash-table-walk t (lambda (k v) (set! from-walk (cons (list k v) from-walk))))\n(hash-table-for-each (lambda (k v) (set! from-for-each (cons (list k v) from-for-each))) t)\n(display (list 'identical (equal? from-walk from-for-each)))\n(newline)\n(define got (reverse from-walk))\n(display (list 'visits (length got) 'size (hash-table-size t) 'exactly-once (= (length got) (hash-table-size t))))\n(newline)\n(display (list 'keys-match (equal? (map car got) (hash-table-keys t))\n               'values-match (equal? (map cadr got) (hash-table-values t))))\n(newline)"
     :invariant "∀ h proc. walk(h, proc) ≡ for-each(proc, h) ∧ the visit sequence = zip(keys(h), values(h)) ∧ |visits| = size(h) ∧ repeated traversals of an unmutated table agree"
     :meta "The pattern is spelling-equivalence, and it is worth proving explicitly precisely because it is assumed everywhere. Two separately-defined bindings can drift — one could be updated for a bug and the other missed — and the only cheap defence is a test that runs both and compares the whole visit sequence rather than a summary of it. Comparing sequences and not just counts is what catches a divergence in ORDER, which is the drift most likely to go unnoticed. The order claim is Motoi-specific; SRFI-125 promises no order, so on a conforming implementation only the multiset equality would survive. What would falsify: the two visit lists differing, a visit count differing from the size, or the sequence disagreeing with hash-table-keys. See book-of-code ch09 on traversal contracts."
     :eng "The engineering payoff is that a file can be migrated from one spelling to the other mechanically, and a single test comparing the two traversals guards the migration."
     :math "The claim is that the two procedures are related by the flip combinator: walk = flip(for-each). Flip preserves everything except argument position, so all the traversal properties — coverage, non-duplication, order — are shared automatically once the equivalence is established."
     :cs "This is an API-surface equivalence, the kind that should ideally be enforced by defining one in terms of the other. Motoi defines them separately for clarity in the source, which is exactly why the equivalence deserves a runnable test rather than a comment.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define save-file (make-hash-table))\n(define world (hash-table 'player (list 10 20) 'chest (list 40 5) 'door (list 0 0)))\n(hash-table-walk world (lambda (k v) (hash-table-set! save-file k (list 'at (car v) (cadr v)))))\n(hash-table-walk save-file (lambda (k v) (display (list k v)) (newline)))\n(display (list 'saved (hash-table-size save-file) 'of (hash-table-size world)))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (fold-via-walk t f seed)\n  (let ((acc seed))\n    (hash-table-walk t (lambda (k v) (set! acc (f k v acc))))\n    acc))\n(define t (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3 'd 4))\n(display (list 'sum-via-walk (fold-via-walk t (lambda (k v acc) (+ acc v)) 0)\n               'sum-via-fold (hash-table-fold t (lambda (k v acc) (+ acc v)) 0)\n               'agree (= (fold-via-walk t (lambda (k v acc) (+ acc v)) 0)\n                         (hash-table-fold t (lambda (k v acc) (+ acc v)) 0))\n               'keys-via-walk (reverse (fold-via-walk t (lambda (k v acc) (cons k acc)) (list)))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (table->report t title)\n  (let ((lines (list)))\n    (hash-table-walk t (lambda (k v) (set! lines (cons (string-append (symbol->string k) \"=\" (number->string v)) lines))))\n    (list title (reverse lines) (length lines))))\n(display (table->report (hash-table 'hp 30 'mp 12 'gold 7) 'status))\n(newline)"
     :base "hash-table-walk applies a two-argument procedure to every association, table first."
     :composition "(define (fold-via-walk t f seed)\n  (let ((acc seed))\n    (hash-table-walk t (lambda (k v) (set! acc (f k v acc))))\n    acc))\n(define t (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3 'd 4))\n(display (fold-via-walk t (lambda (k v acc) (+ acc v)) 0))\n(newline)\n(display (hash-table-fold t (lambda (k v acc) (+ acc v)) 0))\n(newline)\n(display (reverse (fold-via-walk t (lambda (k v acc) (cons k acc)) (list))))\n(newline)"
     :emergent "fold rebuilt from walk — an effectful traversal plus one captured binding reconstructs the value-returning traversal, which shows that fold is not a separate capability but a discipline imposed on a walk"
     :meta "The composition chains hash-table-walk (visit everything) with set! on a binding captured by the enclosing let. What emerges is fold: because the accumulator lives outside the procedure and each visit replaces it with a function of itself, the sequence of effects becomes a sequence of values, and the last one is the answer. The demonstration matters because it establishes a hierarchy — walk is the primitive, fold is walk plus a discipline, and hash-table-count and hash-table->alist are folds with particular arguments. That also explains why hash-table-fold is preferable in practice despite being derivable: the captured binding is exactly what a reader has to reason about, and fold's signature promises there is only one and that it is threaded correctly. The same family appears as reduce built on foreach in any imperative language, as an accumulator variable in a for loop, as a running aggregate in a cursor loop. See book-of-composition ch09 on deriving fold from iteration.")
)
)
